### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that the claim is both scientifically inaccurate and a misrepresentation of Professor Paul Lehner's comments. Professor Lehner did not advocate for "pox parties" as a "good way" to build herd immunity, but rather predicted them as a potential "last resort" parents might turn to. Scientifically, experts and public health officials have thoroughly rejected the idea of achieving herd immunity through natural infection, labeling it a "dangerous fallacy" that is "scientifically and ethically problematic." There is no evidence of lasting immunity from natural infection, especially with the rise of rapidly mutating variants. Furthermore, deliberately exposing children to the virus carries severe medical risks—such as Multisystem Inflammatory Syndrome (MIS-C) and Long COVID—and would lead to high transmission rates, overwhelmed healthcare systems, and increased mortality.

### Conclusion
Based on the comprehensive evidence from the fact-check, the claim that deliberately infecting children at "pox parties" is a good way to build herd immunity is entirely false. The scientist to whom the claim is attributed did not recommend this practice, and the global scientific community has clearly established that attempting to reach herd immunity through natural infection is highly dangerous, ineffective, and medically irresponsible. Therefore, the decision is that the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because Professor Paul Lehner did not advocate for "pox parties," but rather predicted them as a potential "last resort" scenario in a [Daily Mail](https://web.archive.org/web/20201201202446/https://www.dailymail.co.uk/news/article-8868269/Controversial-pox-parties-used-build-immunity-Covid-19-scientist-says.html) article. Furthermore, scientific experts warn that achieving herd immunity through natural infection is a "dangerous fallacy" because there is no evidence of lasting protective immunity, and rapidly mutating variants make it highly unlikely ([NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/covid-19-herd-immunity-backed-white-house-dangerous-fallacy-scientists-n1243415), [Healthline](https://www.healthline.com/health/herd-immunity)). Deliberately infecting children also poses severe medical risks, such as Multisystem Inflammatory Syndrome (MIS-C) and Long COVID ([Mayo Clinic](https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/coronavirus/in-depth/coronavirus-in-babies-and-children/art-20484405)), and infected children carry high viral loads that can easily transmit the virus to vulnerable community members ([Harvard Medical School](https://hms.harvard.edu/news/silent-spreaders)). Ultimately, attempting to reach herd immunity through natural infection would overwhelm hospitals and cause high mortality rates ([Johns Hopkins University](https://hub.jhu.edu/2020/04/30/herd-immunity-covid-19-coronavirus/)).